(41, 19, 'A', 'A single string value', 0, 'Not quite. A list is a collection of items in a particular order.'),
(42, 19, 'B', 'A mathematical operation', 0, 'Not quite. A list is a collection of items in a particular order.'),
(43, 19, 'C', 'A quotation mark', 0, 'Not quite. A list is a collection of items in a particular order.'),
(44, 19, 'D', 'A collection of items in a particular order', 1, 'Correct! A list is a collection of items in a particular order.'),
(45, 20, 'A', 'That the file is empty', 0, 'Good try! Keep going, you\'re getting closer! The extension isn\'t about the file being empty — it tells your editor and system that the file contains a Python program.'),
(46, 20, 'B', 'That the file should be deleted', 0, 'Nice effort! You can get this one next time. .py doesn\'t signal deletion — it identifies the file as containing Python code.'),
(47, 20, 'C', 'That the file cannot be edited', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! A .py file can absolutely be edited — the extension simply marks it as Python code'),
(48, 20, 'D', 'That the file contains a Python program', 1, 'Correct! The lesson explains that the .py file extension tells your editor and system that the file contains a Python program.'),
(49, 21, 'A', 'It deletes any lines it doesn\'t understand', 0, 'Good try! Keep going, you\'re getting closer! The interpreter doesn\'t delete lines — it reads the file line-by-line to figure out what each part means'),
(50, 21, 'B', 'It reads the file line-by-line to figure out what each part means', 1, 'Correct! The lesson explains that your editor runs the file through the Python interpreter, which reads it line-by-line to figure out what each part means'),
(51, 21, 'C', 'It converts the file into a different file extension', 0, 'Nice effort! You can get this one next time. The interpreter doesn\'t change the file extension — it processes the code to determine what it means.'),
(52, 21, 'D', 'It only reads the last line of the file', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! The interpreter reads the whole file line-by-line, not just the last line.'),
(53, 22, 'A', 'It deletes the text inside the parentheses', 0, 'Good try! Keep going, you\'re getting closer! The interpreter doesn\'t delete anything — it displays whatever text is inside the parentheses on the screen.'),
(54, 22, 'B', 'It saves the text to a new file', 0, 'Nice effort! You can get this one next time. print() doesn\'t save to a file — it displays the text on the screen.'),
(55, 22, 'C', 'It displays whatever text is inside those parentheses on the screen', 1, 'Correct! The lesson explains that when the interpreter sees print() followed by parentheses, it displays whatever text is inside those parentheses on the screen.'),
(56, 22, 'D', 'It asks the user to type a response', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! print() doesn\'t collect input — it only displays the given text on the screen.'),
(57, 23, 'A', 'It automatically fixes syntax errors', 0, 'Good try! Keep going, you\'re getting closer! Syntax highlighting doesn\'t fix errors on its own — it color-codes different parts of your code to make errors easier to catch.'),
(58, 23, 'B', 'It deletes unnecessary code automatically', 0, 'Nice effort! You can get this one next time. Syntax highlighting doesn\'t delete code — it applies color-coding to different parts of your code.'),
(59, 23, 'C', 'It runs the code automatically as you type', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! Syntax highlighting doesn\'t execute code — it visually color-codes different parts of your code as you write it.'),
(60, 23, 'D', 'It color-codes different parts of your code', 1, 'Correct! The lesson explains that your code editor automatically applies syntax highlighting, color-coding different parts of your code.'),
(61, 24, 'A', 'To make it easier to catch syntax errors and typos as you write', 1, 'Correct! The lesson explains that this color-coding, such as showing print() as a function and the text as data, makes it easier to catch syntax errors and typos as you write.'),
(62, 24, 'B', 'Because functions and text data must always be different lengths', 0, 'Good try! Keep going, you\'re getting closer! Length isn\'t the reason for the different colors — it\'s about visually distinguishing different types of code elements.'),
(63, 24, 'C', 'Because Python requires different colors to run the code', 0, 'Nice effort! You can get this one next time. Python doesn\'t require specific colors to run code — syntax highlighting is a visual aid in the editor, not a requirement for execution'),
(64, 24, 'D', 'To make the code file smaller', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! Syntax highlighting doesn\'t affect file size — it\'s purely a visual aid for readability and catching errors.'),
(65, 25, 'A', 'A function that displays text', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! a variable isn\'t a function — the lesson defines it as a label that holds a value.'),
(66, 25, 'B', 'A type of error message', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. a variable isn\'t related to error messages — it\'s a label connected to information that python keeps track of.'),
(67, 25, 'C', 'A label that holds a value', 1, 'Correct! the lesson defines a variable as a label that holds a value, connected to information that python always keeps track of.'),
(68, 25, 'D', 'A file extension', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! a variable isn\'t a file extension — file extensions like .py are a separate concept covered earlier'),
(69, 26, 'A', 'It deletes the value immediately', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! assigning a value doesn\'t delete it — python stores the value so it can be used later'),
(70, 26, 'B', 'It stores the value', 1, 'Correct! the lesson explains that when you assign a value to a variable, python stores it.'),
(71, 26, 'C', 'It prints the value automatically', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. assignment alone doesn\'t print anything — a separate print() call is needed to display the value.'),
(72, 26, 'D', 'It converts the value into a different data type', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! assignment doesn\'t change the value\'s data type — it simply stores whatever value was assigned.'),
(73, 27, 'A', 'Python keeps both the old and new values together', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! python doesn\'t keep both values — it replaces the old value with the new one.'),
(74, 27, 'B', 'Python creates a second variable with the same name', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. python doesn\'t create a second variable — it replaces the value stored in the existing one.'),
(75, 27, 'C', 'Python replaces the old value with the new one', 1, 'Correct! the lesson explains that if you update the variable later, python replaces the old value with the new one.'),
(76, 27, 'D', 'Python raises an error', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! updating a variable\'s value doesn\'t cause an error — it\'s a normal operation that replaces the old value.'),
(77, 28, 'A', 'The word \"message\"', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! print(message) doesn\'t display the variable\'s name — it displays the value currently stored in message.'),
(78, 28, 'B', 'Hello python world!\"', 1, 'Correct! print(message) displays the value currently stored in message, which is \"hello python world!\".'),
(79, 28, 'C', 'Nothing, since message hasn\'t been printed yet', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. print(message) does display something — specifically, the value stored in message at that point.'),
(80, 28, 'D', 'An error, since message isn\'t defined', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! message is defined on the line above, so print(message) runs successfully rather than causing an error.'),
(81, 29, 'A', 'Yes, a new variable must always be created', 1, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! the common mistake note explicitly says this isn\'t true — you don\'t need to create a new variable to change a value.'),
(82, 29, 'B', 'No, you just assign the variable again', 0, 'Correct! the common mistake note explains that you don\'t need to \"create\" a new variable to change its value — you just assign it again, and python replaces what was there before.'),
(83, 29, 'C', 'Yes, but only for text values', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. this isn\'t limited to certain value types — reassigning works the same way regardless of what kind of value is stored.'),
(84, 29, 'D', 'No, variables can never be changed once assigned', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! variables absolutely can be changed — the lesson\'s second example shows message being reassigned to a new value.'),
(85, 30, 'A', 'A backup copy of your code', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! variable names aren\'t limited to only letters — the lesson also allows numbers and underscores.'),
(86, 30, 'B', 'A tool for renaming variables automatically', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. dashes aren\'t allowed — the lesson specifically says to avoid special symbols or dashes.'),
(87, 30, 'C', 'Python\'s error report', 1, 'Correct! the lesson refers to python\'s error reports as tracebacks, which make fixing mistakes quick and easy once you learn to read them.'),
(88, 30, 'D', 'A saved version of a previous program', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! not any symbol is allowed — special symbols and dashes should be avoided.'),
(89, 31, 'A', 'Using too many variables in one program', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! a variable name must never start with a number — that\'s explicitly called out as not allowed.'),
(90, 31, 'B', 'Trying to use a variable that python doesn\'t recognize', 0, 'Correct! the lesson explains that a nameerror usually occurs when you try to use a variable that python doesn\'t recognize.'),
(91, 31, 'C', 'Printing too much text at once', 1, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. a variable name can start with either a letter or an underscore, not exclusively an underscore.'),
(92, 31, 'D', 'Using lowercase letters in a variable name', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! special symbols aren\'t allowed to start a variable name — only a letter or underscore is permitted.'),
(93, 32, 'A', 'The file name and line number only', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! dashes aren\'t used for this — the lesson specifies using underscores instead of spaces.'),
(94, 32, 'B', 'The exact line of code that failed', 0, 'Correct! the lesson explains that the \"problematic line\" section displays the exact line of code that failed, such as print(mesage).'),
(95, 32, 'C', 'A list of every variable in the program', 1, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. periods aren\'t mentioned as a way to represent spaces — underscores are the correct choice'),
(96, 32, 'D', 'A suggestion for renaming the entire file', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! spaces shouldn\'t be left in variable names — the lesson specifies replacing them with underscores.'),
(97, 33, 'A', 'Yes, python corrects misspelled english words automatically', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! keyword casing isn\'t the reason given — the lesson simply lists reserved words as names to avoid.'),
(98, 33, 'B', 'No, python only enforces exact consistency', 1, 'Correct! the lesson lists \"reserved words\" as a rule, explaining not to use python keywords like print as variable names.'),
(99, 33, 'C', 'Yes, but only for variable names longer than 5 letters', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. that\'s not the reason given — the lesson simply states that reserved words like print shouldn\'t be used as variable names.'),
(100, 33, 'D', 'No, python refuses to run any misspelled code', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! keywords don\'t start with numbers — the concern is that they\'re reserved words, not related to starting characters.'),
(101, 34, 'A', 'Because python automatically corrects the spelling', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! the note doesn\'t say these cause immediate errors — it warns that they\'re easy to confuse with 1 and 0.'),
(102, 34, 'B', 'Because mesage is a real english word', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. python doesn\'t treat these letters as numbers — the concern is visual confusion between the characters, not automatic conversion.'),
(103, 34, 'C', 'Because both lines use the exact same spelling, mesage', 1, 'Correct! the lesson explains that both lines have the exact same typo (mesage), so the code runs successfully because python only requires exact consistency, not correct english.'),
(104, 34, 'D', 'Because python ignores all typos in variable names', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! that describes uppercase letters in general, mentioned separately in the note — the common mistake specifically warns about confusing l/o with 1/0.'),
(105, 35, 'A', 'A type of variable name', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! a method isn\'t a variable name — the lesson defines it as an action python performs on a piece of data.'),
(106, 35, 'B', 'An action python performs on a piece of data', 1, 'Correct! the lesson defines a method as an action python performs on a piece of data.'),
(107, 35, 'C', 'A file extension', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. file extensions like .py are unrelated — a method is an action performed on data.'),
(108, 35, 'D', 'A type of error message', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! methods aren\'t error messages — they\'re actions performed on data, like changing a string\'s case.'),
(109, 36, 'A', 'Delete the variable name', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! the dot doesn\'t delete anything — it directs python to run the title() method on the string value inside name.'),
(110, 36, 'B', 'Run the title() method on the string value inside name', 1, 'Correct! the lesson explains that the dot after name in name.title() directs python to run the title() method on the string value inside name.'),
(111, 36, 'C', 'Create a new variable called title', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. no new variable is created — the dot notation applies the title() method to the existing name variable.'),
(112, 36, 'D', 'Convert name into a number', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! .title() doesn\'t convert to a number — it changes the capitalization of the string.'),
(113, 37, 'A', 'Because parentheses are optional and can be removed', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! the lesson states methods are always followed by parentheses — they aren\'t optional, they\'re just empty here.'),
(114, 37, 'B', 'Because case-changing methods don\'t actually run', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. these methods do run and change the string\'s case — the parentheses are simply empty because no extra input is required.'),
(115, 37, 'C', 'Because no extra input is required for case-changing methods', 1, 'Correct! the lesson explains that for case-changing methods, no extra input is required, so the parentheses remain empty.'),
(116, 37, 'D', 'Because parentheses are only used for numbers', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! parentheses aren\'t limited to numbers — they follow methods in general, holding extra instructions when needed.'),
(117, 38, 'A', 'Capitalizes only the first letter of the string', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! that describes .title() applied to a single word — .upper() converts all letters to uppercase.'),
(118, 38, 'B', 'Removes all letters from the string', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. .upper() doesn\'t remove letters — it converts all of them to uppercase.'),
(119, 38, 'C', 'Converts all letters to uppercase', 1, 'Correct! the lesson defines .upper() as converting all letters to uppercase, turning \"ada lovelace\" into \"ada lovelace\".'),
(120, 38, 'D', 'Converts all letters to lowercase', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! that describes .lower() — .upper() does the opposite, converting all letters to uppercase.'),
(121, 39, 'A', 'To make the text print faster', 1, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! speed isn\'t the reason given — the note explains that .lower() ensures consistency in your database.'),
(122, 39, 'B', 'To ensure consistency in your database, since user input capitalization can\'t be trusted', 0, 'Correct! the common mistake note explains that you often can\'t trust the capitalization provided by user inputs, so converting to lowercase with .lower() before storing ensures consistency.'),
(123, 39, 'C', 'Because .title() and .upper() don\'t work on user input', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. .title() and .upper() do work on user input — the note explains they can be applied later, when displaying information back to the user.'),
(124, 39, 'D', 'Because uppercase letters cause nameerrors', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! this isn\'t about nameerrors — the note is about ensuring consistent storage of user-provided text, regardless of how it was capitalized when entered.'),
(125, 40, 'A', 'Because one uses single quotes and the other uses double quotes', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! both use single quotes here — the difference is the extra trailing space in \'python \'.'),
(126, 40, 'B', 'Because of the extra space at the end of one of them', 1, 'Correct! the lesson explains that to python, \'python\' and \'python \' are treated as two distinct strings because of the extra space.'),
(127, 40, 'C', 'Because one is a number and the other is text', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. both are strings (text) — the distinction is purely the extra whitespace, not a data type difference.'),
(128, 40, 'D', 'Because python randomly assigns different values', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! this isn\'t random — the difference comes specifically from the extra trailing space.'),
(129, 41, 'A', 'The left side', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! that describes .lstrip() — .rstrip() removes trailing whitespace from the end (right side) of a string.'),
(130, 41, 'B', 'The right side', 1, 'Correct! the lesson states that .rstrip()\'s target area is the right side, removing trailing whitespace from the end of a string.'),
(131, 41, 'C', 'Both sides', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. that describes .strip() — .rstrip() only targets the right side.'),
(132, 41, 'D', 'The middle of the string', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! none of these stripping methods target the middle — .rstrip() specifically targets the right side.'),
(133, 42, 'A', '.rstrip()', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! .rstrip() only removes whitespace from the right side — .strip() targets both sides.'),
(134, 42, 'B', '.lstrip()', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. .lstrip() only removes whitespace from the left side — .strip() targets both sides.'),
(135, 42, 'C', '.title()', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! .title() changes capitalization, not whitespace — .strip() is the method that removes whitespace from both sides.'),
(136, 42, 'D', 'Strip()', 1, 'Correct! the lesson states that .strip()\'s target area is both sides, removing whitespace simultaneously from both the start and end of a string.'),
(137, 43, 'A', 'Yes, it always changes the variable permanently', 1, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! the lesson explains that calling a stripping method only returns a stripped copy temporarily — it does not alter the original stored variable.'),
(138, 43, 'B', 'No, it only returns a stripped copy temporarily', 0, 'Correct! the lesson states that calling a stripping method on a variable only returns a stripped copy temporarily; it does not alter the original stored variable.'),
(139, 43, 'C', 'Yes, but only if .strip() is used instead', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. even .strip() only returns a copy — permanence requires reassigning the result back to the variable, regardless of which stripping method is used.'),
(140, 43, 'D', 'No, stripping methods never affect the string at all', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! stripping methods do affect the returned value — they just don\'t change the original variable unless that result is reassigned back to it.'),
(141, 44, 'A', 'By calling .strip() twice in a row', 1, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! calling a stripping method multiple times doesn\'t make it permanent — permanence requires reassigning the result back to the variable.'),
(142, 44, 'B', 'By using print() instead of .strip()', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. print() doesn\'t make a change permanent — it only displays a value; the fix is reassigning the stripped result back to the variable.'),
(143, 44, 'C', 'By defining a brand-new variable with a different name', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! the lesson\'s example reassigns the same variable, favorite_language = favorite_language.rstrip(), rather than creating a new one.'),
(144, 44, 'D', 'By reassigning the stripped result back to the variable', 0, 'Correct! the lesson explains that to make the change permanent, you must reassign the stripped result back to the variable.'),
(145, 45, 'A', 'Using print()', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! the lesson does not identify print() as the cause.'),
(146, 45, 'B', 'Using double quotes correctly', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. double quotes are shown as the correct way to safely use an apostrophe inside a string.'),
(147, 45, 'C', 'Using strings', 0, 'You\'re on the right track, keep it up! the issue is mismatched or misplaced quotation marks.'),
(148, 45, 'D', 'Mismatched or misplaced quotation marks', 1, 'Correct! the lesson identifies mismatched or misplaced quotation marks as a common cause.'),
(149, 46, 'A', 'Python removes the apostrophe.', 0, 'Good try! keep going, you\'re getting closer! python interprets the apostrophe as an ending quote mark.'),
(150, 46, 'B', 'Python changes the string.', 0, 'Nice effort! you can get this one next time. the problem occurs because python interprets the apostrophe as the ending quote.'),
